WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.940
When it comes to the creative process, there's
a method to the madness of Avengers: Endgame

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.470
co-directors Joe and Anthony Russo.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:11.669
During a Reddit "ask-me-anything" session,
the ace filmmakers responded to a fan who

00:00:11.669 --> 00:00:15.949
asked why one of the flick's more famous deleted
scenes, in which all of the assembled heroes

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:21.050
take a knee out of respect after Tony Stark's
heroic act of self-sacrifice, wasn't included

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.710
in the final cut of the film.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:27.289
In case you haven't gotten a chance to catch
the excised scene, here's a brief explanation.

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:31.190
You'll recall that Stark mortally injured
himself while using the Infinity Stones to

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:35.990
snap Thanos and his invading armies out of
existence; unable to even speak, he held on

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:40.780
long enough for Peter Parker to tell him that
the Avengers had won, and for his wife, Pepper

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.539
Potts, to reassure him that she and his daughter,
Morgan, would be okay without him.

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:48.070
"You can rest now."

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.629
And so he did rest, taking his last breath.

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:54.289
The deleted scene in question would have taken
place directly after this.

00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:59.120
In it, we first see an utterly shell-shocked
Captain America and Thor, in disbelief over

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.120
the loss.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.800
Then, we cut to Hawkeye, who slowly, tentatively
drops to one knee.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.250
One by one, the rest of the heroes follow
suit: first T'Challa, then Captain Marvel,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:14.160
followed by Peter Quill, Nebula, Valkyrie,
Ant-Man, Cap, and Doctor Strange.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.150
As the camera pulls out to a wide shot, we
see that everybody is kneeling, except, of

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:24.470
course, for 2014 Gamora, who has taken the
opportunity to slip away without anyone noticing.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.890
The scene is absolutely fraught with emotion,
and would have fit right in with the finished

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.810
movie, an idea the Russos didn't disagree
with.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.720
According to the directors, the problem was
that as the scene played out, they noticed

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.000
it was quite similar to another scene which
they had planned for the flick's conclusion,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
one that was just as epic, and which did make
the final cut.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.000
They explained:

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.590
"We found the Avengers kneeling scene incredibly
emotional, [but] it felt incredibly similar

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.480
to the moment when all of the heroes stood
on the shore at Tony's funeral, paying their

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.070
respects to him outside his home.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.420
The two scenes, when in the film together,
seemed to fight one another, and created a

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.030
repetitious ending."

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.950
That actually makes a ton of sense; the way
in which the camera lingers on every character

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.790
in the deleted sequence, making sure that
everyone's reaction is recorded, strikes pretty

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.780
much the exact same emotional chords as the
funeral scene.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.770
It could also be argued that the funeral scene
is more impressive because it's strikingly

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:20.019
accomplished in a single shot, and because
there are a few more relevant parties present

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:24.390
such as Nick Fury, Happy Hogan, and a grown-up
Harley Keener.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:28.349
If you'll recall, he was the kid who helped
Stark out during the events of Iron Man 3.

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:32.170
"I would've added in the retro…"

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.170
"Retro-reflexive panels?"

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:34.170
"To make him stealth mode."

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.170
"You want a stealth mode?"

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.170
"Cool, right?"

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.170
"That's actually a good idea.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.170
Maybe I'll build one."

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:41.099
It's easy to see how the inclusion of the
"Take a Knee" scene could have diluted the

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:43.879
emotional impact of that dramatic one-take
sequence.

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:47.900
It's worth asking, though, if the scene might
show up in an alternate version of Endgame

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.959
down the road...a director's cut, perhaps?

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:54.810
It turns out that we have an answer to that
question as well, thanks to the Russos' AMA.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.959
Their answer to the question of whether or
not there would ever be a director's cut of

00:02:57.959 --> 00:02:59.640
Endgame was straightforward.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:00.640
They said:

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.390
"Honestly no, we have always released the
version of [each of our movies] we wanted

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:05.390
to.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.830
So you would never see a director's cut of
a Russo Brothers' Marvel movie, because you

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.260
have already seen it."

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.950
We would have expected no less.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.569
The directing duo earned the trust of Marvel
Studios with their first MCU effort, 2014's

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:20.629
Captain America: The Winter Soldier, which
is widely considered to be one of the best

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:22.609
movies the company has produced.

00:03:22.609 --> 00:03:26.269
They were then handed the task of fielding
three of the most complex crossover films

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:30.819
ever produced in Captain America: Civil War
and the one-two punch of Avengers: Infinity

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:32.499
War and Endgame.

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:35.580
These guys obviously know what they're doing,
and although they've said that their run with

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.939
Marvel has pretty much concluded, it so happens
that they've also expressed interest in a

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:44.260
couple of properties which are now under the
studio's control: the Fantastic Four and the

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.260
X-Men.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.810
After giving Marvel the biggest movie of all
time, we're going to go out on a limb and

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:52.180
say that they just might be down to work with
these two again.

